        {
            "number": 4,
            "revision": "86b048ab914a89b5dfa3fad77fa46518b270d3c7",
            "parents": [
                "13289c9414b6838eaa8544cbfc393b5f2ef9672b"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/58/80158/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Chandra Harkude",
                "email": "Chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com",
                "username": "chandramohan83"
            },
            "createdOn": 1747322724,
            "author": {
                "name": "Chandramohan Harkude",
                "email": "chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com",
                "username": ""
            },
            "kind": "NO_CODE_CHANGE",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "why we need this MaxPasswordLength configurable?\nI think we are good with MAX_PASSWORD_LENGTH meson flag with default value 64"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Chandra Harkude",
                        "email": "Chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com",
                        "username": "chandramohan83"
                    },
                    "message": "NIST https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html guideline says\nVerifiers and CSPs SHOULD permit a maximum password length of at least 64 characters.\n\nThis indicates 64 is the least number for max password length and security policies must be allowed to configure higher number. Therefore I kept the type as size so that higher number can be configured."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "@Chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com\n\nYou didn't answer this:\n\n> why we need this MaxPasswordLength configurable?\n\nCan we just change the meson flag to 255?  Or 1024?  Does PAM have a limit?\n\nI don't really understand why you'd want to configure a limit below what PAM supports, unless this is driven by what IPMI supports?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Chandra Harkude",
                        "email": "Chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com",
                        "username": "chandramohan83"
                    },
                    "message": "Making MaxPasswordLength configurable allows system admin to enforce stricter password policies\n\nCan\u2019t we just set the meson flag to 255 or 1024 and be done?\n \u2022 Yes, you can raise the Meson build-time default (e.g., --Dmax-password-length=1024). That would increase the default upper bound.\n    However:\n\u2022Without configurability, you force all systems to accept that high limit \u2014 which may not be desirable for all platforms.\n\u2022You lose flexibility to tighten policies dynamically.\n\nDoes PAM have a limit?\n  \u2022PAM doesn\u2019t explicitly define a max password length across all modules, but Many PAM modules use standard libc functions, which may impose a limit (commonly 512 bytes or more, sometimes unbounded).\n  \nWhy configure a limit below what PAM supports\n  \u2022Security Policy Enforcement : Some organizations prefer password complexity over length and want to restrict overly long passwords"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> Security Policy Enforcement : Some organizations prefer password complexity over length and want to restrict overly long passwords\n\nDo you have evidence of this?  Doing so explicitly violates the NIST guidelines you are saying you are trying to improve support for.\n\nThe following statements are present in the linked NIST guidelines:\n\n> Other complexity requirements for passwords SHALL NOT be imposed.\n\n> Verifiers and CSPs SHALL NOT impose other composition rules (e.g., requiring mixtures of different character types) for passwords.\n\n> Users should be encouraged to make their passwords as lengthy as they want, within reason. Since the size of a hashed password is independent of its length, there is no reason to prohibit the use of lengthy passwords (or passphrases) if the user wishes. Extremely long passwords (perhaps megabytes long) could require excessive processing time to hash, so it is reasonable to have some limit."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Chandra Harkude",
                        "email": "Chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com",
                        "username": "chandramohan83"
                    },
                    "message": "As you said as per https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html#length \nit says it is reasonable to have some limit.\n\nif user/admin wants to change this limit dynamically, we need to make it configurable.\n\nThe guidelines no where explicitly says that max password can't be configured dyanamically.\n\nRight now I don't have the use case to support this dyanamic configuration but I want to provide the flexibility to admin/organization."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "> if user/admin wants to change this limit dynamically, we need to make it configurable.\n\nWhy?  This is not logically connected. We haven\u2019t had it configurable before. Why should we start now?  This seems like low value code to me.\n\nJust because something _could_ be configured doesn\u2019t mean we _have_ allow it to be configured.  We already support 64, which is within the guidelines of NIST. We can extend it with a 1 line change, which is still in the guidelines of NIST. If we allow users to reduce it we both have more code to maintain _and_ we start to violate NISTs guidelines.  This sounds like a lose-lose to me.\n\n> it says it is reasonable to have some limit.\n\n\u201cReasonable\u201d in the words of the document was \u201cdon\u2019t allow it to be so long that hashing speed is noticeable\u201d.  We are talking about 10s of kb at least before this even becomes a concern.  64, 256, 1024; these are all \u201creasonable\u201d and within the bounds of NIST."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Chandra Harkude",
                        "email": "Chandramohan.harkude@gmail.com",
                        "username": "chandramohan83"
                    },
                    "message": "Okay will abandonthe MR"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/User/AccountPolicy.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 33,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Just to be clear, this isn\u2019t a \u201cyou may not do this upstream\u201d statement. I\u2019m trying to understand the motivation. We need the motivation recorded so that someone later doesn\u2019t pull it back out \u201cfor simplicity\u201d. The motivation isn\u2019t clear nor articulated."
                },
                {
